is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: OSPF default route origination at the Internet edge router (r1), specifically FRR’s OSPF process depending on the kernel RIB.
- Mechanism: Internal routing is fine (OSPF neighbors Full, inter-office pings succeed), but no device in the OSPF domain has a default route to the Internet. When hosts send traffic to public IPs, their first-hop L3 gateway has no 0.0.0.0/0, so it returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” (as seen from pc1: “From 10.1.0.2”). OSPF “default-information originate” on r1 is configured, but FRR only advertises a default if one exists in the RIB.
- Localization: On r1, there is no default route in the kernel table (ip route on r1: no 0.0.0.0/0 present). Consequently, downstream routers (csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2) also have no 0.0.0.0/0 (show ip route 0.0.0.0/0 returns empty). r1 can ping the ISP peers 203.0.113.1 and 203.0.113.5 because those are directly connected /30s, masking the absence of a default.
- Root cause: r1 lacks a default route to the ISP(s), so OSPF never originates a default LSA and internal gateways have no path for Internet-bound traffic.
- Corrective fix: Install a default route on r1 toward the ISP next-hops (e.g., “ip route add default nexthop via 203.0.113.1 nexthop via 203.0.113.5” or in FRR “ip route 0.0.0.0/0 203.0.113.1” plus a second for 203.0.113.5). Alternatively, use “default-information originate always” in OSPF. Verify that csw/dsw tables show 0.0.0.0/0 via r1 and that PCs can ping 203.0.113.1 successfully.

=== END ===